93
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:39,920
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

94
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:52,680
There's lots of different styles of leadership out there,

95
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:56,920
And I'm going to oversimplify things

96
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:02,840
And I'm going to take a moment and look at two different styles of leadership,

97
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:04,960
two different troops:

98
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:08,360
One is led by adults and one is led by scouts.

99
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:10,760
Now, what's the difference between those two?

100
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:13,680
Is there any just like basic difference?

101
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:15,440
There's lots of differences between them,

102
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:19,800
but why is one led by adults and one led by scouts?

103
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:24,080
I'm going to offer you this again, at the risk of oversimplifying things.

104
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:26,680
The answer is focus.

105
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:32,159
I think that most troops run by adults are focused on results

106
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:36,080
and troops run by scouts are focused on process.

107
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:40,280
I accept that between the two extremes of fully adult and fully youth led,

108
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:42,000
there got to be a lot of shades of gray,

109
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,200
but I think the basic conclusion of focus holds up.

110
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:49,840
Now, as I've said many times, adults are more experienced

111
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:53,040
and more capable of producing results, of posting good numbers.

112
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:57,880
I'd readily admit that if you want reliable results and good numbers,

113
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:01,680
it's best to have the adults lead things. in a lot of ways.

114
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:04,400
If we're focused on the process of scouting,

115
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:06,480
we're going to be more or less youth led.

116
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:07,480
Well, what's that process?

117
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:09,840
It's that set of opportunities and experiences

118
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,000
that shape the live and characters of scouts,

119
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:15,400
without paying a whole lot of attention to the results.

120
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:20,080
Now, if we focus on that process,

121
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:26,280
we will see both the good results and we will also be youth led,

122
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:31,480
And the good results come from the youth leadership.

123
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:34,600
The good results come from the fact

124
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:38,720
that boys are going through the process of leading themselves.

125
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:42,200
The pivot point between the two approaches to scouting

126
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:44,600
is whether or not we understand and value the process.

127
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:49,600
Understanding and valuing results is easy.

128
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:52,520
I mean, you know, hey, we had this many guys advance,

129
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:55,160
we had this many guys go camping. we blah, blah, blah, blah.

130
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:58,880
You know, we do requirements, we earn badges, we go camping,

131
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:01,760
we rack up nights camping and we're in good shape.

132
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:04,480
We recruit scouts and we grow a number.

133
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,720
Hey, that's easy to understand.

134
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:09,960
Understanding and valuing the process behind scouting.

135
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:13,800
the process is quite a bit more subtle.

136
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:16,200
When scouts set their own course,

137
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:18,440
when they learn to work together to make things happen,

138
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:21,520
they're participating in the process of scouting.

139
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:25,080
The results of the process aren't easy to measure.

140
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:28,240
We can't express them numerically

141
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:32,600
And it takes kind of a trained or conditioned eye

142
00:08:33,039 --> 00:08:34,440
to be able to detect them.

143
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:37,600
So if this is right, if the difference

144
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:42,000
between youth lead and adult lead is really a matter of focus,

145
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,240
here's my challenge for you.

146
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:47,080
I want you to start to focus on the process

147
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:52,120
of how things are being done rather than just the results.

148
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:57,440
Once again, adults are more talented and experienced.

149
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:01,120
They'll always be able to get better results than scouts in many ways.

150
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:08,480
How much of your troop is totally in the hands of your scouts?

151
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:12,840
Now, Baden Powell said something along the lines of:

152
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:15,800
you know, the patrol method and this youth leadership thing

153
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:18,920
isn't there just to make life easy on the scout leaders,

154
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:21,440
you know, but it does.

155
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:24,880
Here's an example.

156
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:27,400
I had an email from somebody this week who gave me,

157
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:30,200
you know, a list of four or five different

158
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:34,000
difficulties they were have in being the outings coordinator-

159
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,200
whatever it's called- in their in their troop,

160
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:41,480
And this was a list of difficulties that they were having with parents

161
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,960
who offered to drive scouts to and from outings.

162
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,560
You know, parents wanted to kind of cherry pick

163
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:49,200
which scouts they got to have in their car

164
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:53,440
And some parents only wanted to drive their son

165
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:55,160
because they were going to be staying the weekend.

166
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:57,280
They only wanted to drive their son to the outing.

167
00:09:58,079 --> 00:10:00,760
Some parents kind of asserted their own schedule over the whole thing,

168
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:02,839
saying well, we have to leave on Saturday morning,

169
00:10:02,839 --> 00:10:07,000
And that stranded some boys who didn't have. you know what I'm talking about.

170
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,600
It's kind of a pain, that logistical piece

171
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:13,160
of getting people back and forth and everything.

172
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:16,240
And so this fellow wrote in and he asked my advice on it

173
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:17,000
And you know what

174
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,640
I didn't really have. I didn't have any advice for him

175
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:21,480
because I got to tell you something:

176
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:25,959
It's been years and years and years since I had any idea of who was driving

177
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:32,240
who, or indeed any adult had much of an idea about who was driving who. my part

178
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:38,400
of the whole transportation process, the adult role of the whole transportation

179
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:43,240
process, is basically: I asked the senior patrol leader the Monday

180
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:46,920
before we're going somewhere if he has a trans, if he's assured himself

181
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:49,480
that all his patrol leaders have a transportation plan.

182
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:52,320
Here's what I want to point out.

183
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:57,760
So, given this idea of we need so many seats to get so many boys back

184
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:02,840
and forth to a camp out, you know an adult can manage that

185
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:05,040
And they can manage that pretty easily.

186
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:09,600
Now they're going to have some difficulties, as was reflected in this email,

187
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:11,680
But they can do it.

188
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:15,560
And if the result that you're looking for is to get boys to the camp out,

189
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:17,520
have adults do it.

190
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:21,440
The other way to look at this is that if youth leaders go through this process,

191
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:25,760
they're benefiting and they are also fulfilling this kind of practical

192
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:27,680
thing that needs to happen,

193
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:28,920
But they're benefiting from this.

194
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:31,120
They have to talk to adults.

195
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:35,880
That's kind of scary for a lot of patrol leaders and a lot of senior patrol leaders.

196
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:40,040
They have to talk to these adults and they have to line up rides

197
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:43,840
and they have to tell them- the adults- what to do.

198
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:46,040
But it's a great process for them to go through.

199
00:11:46,040 --> 00:11:49,440
They really grow and learn from just going through that process.

200
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:50,560
What do they grow and learn about?

201
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:52,360
Well, you know what?

202
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:54,560
It's very, very difficult to quantify

203
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:57,960
And it's a real good example of what I've been talking about.

204
00:11:57,960 --> 00:11:59,440
You know what's the focus.

205
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:03,160
If our focus is on results- adults-

206
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:06,120
If our focus is on process- youth-

207
00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:11,880
And you know, I know that some of you are going to question my sanity

208
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:14,200
in trusting something like this to youth,

209
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:16,880
But I got to tell you it's been 15 years-

210
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:23,960
Easy at least, since I've really understood or known who's going to drive, who

211
00:12:23,960 --> 00:12:27,000
You know I get to. we meet at a church

212
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:32,000
And so we leave, usually on troop outings, from the church

213
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,040
And I get there on a Friday and I watch what happens.

214
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:41,960
My senior patrol leader is in charge and his scribe has the roster

215
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:46,320
And he's working with patrol leaders and they're getting gear into cars

216
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:47,080
and things like that

217
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:51,720
And sometimes it's long and painful and it's in fits and starts,

218
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:55,720
But those scouts are going through this process

219
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:02,280
And the process is more important than the immediate result.

220
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:04,640
So well, that's just some food for thought.

221
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:05,560
I hope it makes sense

