247
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:42,880
And congratulations on being a scout master.

248
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:43,880
It's a great ride.

249
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:44,880
You're going to love it.

250
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:48,880
Many of my scouts over the years have had some sort of learning difference or have

251
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:51,880
been identified having various levels of autism.

252
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:55,880
I've got to tell you we don't really end up taking much notice of it because it's

253
00:14:55,880 --> 00:14:59,000
never really been much of an issue.

254
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:04,000
One of the things I have found is that- quote unquote- normal boys- I understand

255
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:09,000
there's no such thing, but boys who don't have this identification are much more

256
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:16,000
accepting and tolerant of things than I was when I was their age.

257
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
It really doesn't seem to be all that big a deal to them.

258
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:27,000
ADHD, you know the whole alphabet thing, and autism and Downs syndrome and things

259
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:31,000
like that. they just seem to take it in stride.

260
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,000
When I was a boy we could be pretty cruel to anyone who didn't think and act like

261
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:41,000
we did, but my scouts are impressively kind and considerate of these differences.

262
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,000
Now parents will usually tell me if they have any special concerns for their

263
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,000
sons or if they have been identified with some kind of clinical difference.

264
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
I ask if there are specific behavior problems that we should watch for,

265
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:01,000
activities or environments that may trigger difficulties for them, and if

266
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
the boys have ever been a danger to himself or others,

267
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
If there are particular areas for concern, well, I'll discuss that with the parent

268
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and then I will also discuss it with my youth leadership,

269
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:15,000
But that's a pretty rare occurrence. to tell you the truth,

270
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:20,000
I feel pretty strongly that we're always best off when a boy defines his journey

271
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
through scouting for himself,

272
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:26,000
And I've had only one or two scouts out of a few hundred that needed any level

273
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,000
of real special consideration.

274
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Most boys, they just seem to thrive in an accepting troop and patrol with

275
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
little or no modifications to what happens.

276
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Principally, the big challenge in this has usually been just working

277
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:48,000
with my adult leaders and, very occasionally, the parents of one of the scouts.

278
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:53,000
The scouts, like I said, I still find it remarkable,

279
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
but they just seem to take it all in stride and make no big deal over it.

280
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Each boy is an individual and each identification or clinical diagnosis

281
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
that a boy has is individual,

282
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,000
And so we have to pay very close attention to that.

283
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Some of these differences in the way that they process the world do create

284
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
behavior problems that could be really disruptive and difficult

285
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:19,000
and could be very off-putting for other scouts

286
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
And we need to know that

287
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
Some of these conditions will also create behavior problems

288
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
where they could be a danger to themselves or others.

289
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
But this is really pretty extraordinarily rare.

290
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
I want to note that but I don't feel it would be responsible for me

291
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
just to say, hey, you know, it's no big deal.

292
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
Everybody goes into the patrol and we see what happens next.

293
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
You do have to be responsible about this

294
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:46,000
and you do have to understand exactly what the clinical identification is.

295
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:50,000
But kind of rule of thumb has been that if a boy is in school

296
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:54,000
and he is right there doing the same thing that everybody else is doing,

297
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
he fits in with scouts really well

298
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
And a lot of times you know what,

299
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
even if they are in special classes or have special considerations in school.

300
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:08,000
all of these clinical determinations are made in an academic setting

301
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and they deal with academic learning

302
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:15,000
And we don't do academics in scouting.

303
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
So it's not that big a deal.

304
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:21,000
I hope that you find that helpful.

305
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:25,000
I certainly don't want to be dismissive of the seriousness

306
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
or the difficulty that a lot of boys do encounter with these things,

307
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:35,000
But at the same time I think that scouting can be a little oasis of calm

308
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
and good for them

309
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:41,000
And they can feel a little less singled out and a little more quote-unquote normal.

310
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
I don't know what normal is.

311
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:46,000
I'm still trying to find out after 52 years.

312
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:53,000
And if you're not familiar with the way that these things are diagnosed

313
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:57,000
or the different measures that are taken to help boys get through them,

314
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
it's a good idea to do a little bit of reading

315
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:04,000
And you'll find that boys in scouting, no matter what their identification,

316
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,000
will always benefit from the time that they spend with their friends,

317
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
the time they spend outside,

318
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:17,000
And you'll have to make minimal adjustments for these different clinical diagnoses.

319
